From: DeepSeek Harness <noreply@deepseek.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 15:04:22 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Move new-folder and upload into a toolbar on the folder's
 path bar, with sort, view, link and refresh, since the actions belong at the
 folder's path rather than under its files; the list's maker row becomes a
 drop-only hint, the grid's last tile a whole new-folder tile, and an empty
 folder keeps its own labelled upload.

---
 AGENTS.md           |   1 +
 CHANGELOG.md        |   1 +
 CLAUDE.md           |   2 +-
 README.md           |   4 +-
 app/browser.go      |  42 +++++++++----
 app/newfolder.go    | 113 ++++++++++++++--------------------
 app/ops_test.go     |  27 +++++---
 app/toolbar_test.go | 147 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 8 files changed, 244 insertions(+), 93 deletions(-)

diff --git a/AGENTS.md b/AGENTS.md
index 3cdcb2b..12b2c86 100644
--- a/AGENTS.md
+++ b/AGENTS.md
@@ -85,6 +85,7 @@ Entering the page reads the cache again only when it is entered (`dedupEnter` no
 - Names are renamed in place (`app/rename.go`), not with `ui.EditableText`: in a `List` it takes the row a click chooses for one chosen before the click, so a plain click on a row starts editing it half a second later. Asking a row's text `Clicked()` (or `Pressed`/`DoubleClicked`) makes it the click's target and the list stops choosing the row; `ObserveInput` watches presses without becoming their target.
 - Virtualized `List` rows get no enter/exit transitions. Staggered entrances use `Keyframes` inside the row (`entering` in `browser.go`).
 - The grid view is not `ui.GridView`: GridView builds each line as an element, so a tile that a resize moves to another line is built anew and jumps. `app/grid.go` positions tiles itself, all under one parent keyed by fsid (percent `Left`/`Width`, so the width never lags), and on a cell change `Jump`s the slot's offset to where it showed and `AnimateTo`s it back to 0. Element `Transition`s don't help here: MyGo skips layout transitions on frames where the window resized.
+- The folder's actions live in a toolbar on the path bar (`app/browser.go`'s `filesToolbar`): a new folder (`folder-plus`), the sort menu, the view switch, the clipboard-link button, refresh, and the upload pill at the end; the folder's own name and search sit before them, and the button's box is where a folder made from it flies from (`listing.makerAt`). The maker (`app/newfolder.go`) is no longer a place for buttons: the grid's last tile is a whole "新建" tile that makes a folder on a click (a plus, or an upload while files are dragged over the window, `listing.over`), and the list's maker row is only the drop hint, in the accent color saying where dropped files will go, built only while files are dragged so it takes no room at the foot of a full folder. An empty folder says "这里还空着" and offers one upload of its own, labelled apart from the toolbar's. A new folder is named in a draft row at the top (`draftID`, put back each frame by `placeDraft`); a click away with its name untouched gives up. Cmd-Shift-N starts it from the toolbar's button box.
 - Theme: the look is a native macOS app's, modeled on Pulse (egoist's MyGo app): the sidebar draws no background so the window's `VibrancySidebar` material shows (`main.go`: `TitleBarHiddenInset`, 52 px title bar), pages marked by white glyphs on colored tiles, the chosen one on a solid accent backdrop, a value at each row's end (`navValue`); the pane is white with a title over a subtitle (`pageTitle`), round gray tool buttons (`toolButton`), capsule segmented controls (`segmented`/`segmentedOf`), which fold into pop-up buttons (`choice`/`popUp`) when the title bar is narrower than `compactBar` (`a.compact`, from its last width; in a folder the tools fold instead whenever its whole path would not fit beside them open, `foldTools`, so the path never folds while they could give way, and the folder's title bar has no subtitle, the status bar counting its items), striped 28 px list rows (`stripe`), rounded gray `group` panels for settings and summaries, and the system typeface (no embedded fonts; `figure` sets tabular digits). Waits that fill a page or stand for a task use `plugins/loading` (a folder's `Ring`, the speed test's `Radar`; the boot screen shows no wait, only the bare window); small ones in buttons, rows and dialogs keep AppKit's `ui.Spinner`. `applyTheme` swaps in the app's light/dark `ui.Theme` each frame. Extra colors are in `palette`. Motion is one curve and three lengths (`motionHover` 120 ms, `motionFade` 160 ms, `motionPanel` 180 ms), plus `popIn` and `rowEnter`, all in `app/theme.go`; no springs or bounce.
 - Shaders (`ui/shader`, sources in `app/shaders/*.kage`, wiring in `app/effects.go`): each shader draw, effect layer or gradient with stops is a "general" GPU instance that the window does not batch, so dozens of them cost frames. Measure with `MYGO_FRAME_STATS=all` (it counts instances by class); draw many small things as plain fills, and keep a shader's per-pixel loop short (`specks.kage` searches only the cells whose specks can reach the pixel, from one hash each, and returns at once outside their reach). Shaders and `Element.Effect` run interpreted on the CPU in `ui.Tester`, so the app uses them only when `a.gpu` (`p.Accelerated()`), with plain fallbacks the tests see.
 - `Painter.AnimationFrame` (and `After`) only paints the window again: the view is not built, so anything computed while building (an element's size, an effect's uniforms, state moved on in `View`) stands still in those frames. Read the time as you paint (`p.Now()` in a `Draw` callback, or in an effect's `PaintEffect`, as `specksFx` does), and call `c.Invalidate()` while something built must move (`a.moving()`). Stamp an animation's start in the first frame that shows it, not from the last frame built, which may be long past.
